From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 09/21] dndcp: Fall back to wl-clipboard without
 ext-data-control-v1

GNOME does not implement ext-data-control-v1. Where the compositor lacks
it, the backend now runs wl-copy to install a host clip and wl-paste to
read the guest selection.

wl-copy serves one payload under one type. The backend gives it the
image if the host sent one, else the plain text, and the RTF only when
there is nothing else, because handing plain text targets the RTF markup
would be worse than losing the formatting. wl-paste picks a text type
itself, so reading text takes one run instead of a type listing and a
read.

The tools run under the same limits as the data control reads. wl-paste
output is polled against the request's deadline and stops at
CPCLIPITEM_MAX_SIZE_V3. wl-copy gets 5 s to install a clip. Each tool
runs in a process group of its own, so that a timeout kills it together
with the process it forked. Nothing counts selection changes on this
path, so every host request reads the selection.

On GNOME both tools map a small window to be given focus. The window
shows in the dock and takes the focus for a moment, and wl-copy waits
for that focus before it installs the selection. The next patch uses the
X selection instead where there is an X display. Without wl-copy, a
warning at start-up says that host to guest copy and paste is off.
